```ts
// This triple slash directive doesn't seem to be recognized by TypeScript on compile time so we can't use this instead of `as any`.
// /// <reference lib="DOM.AsyncIterable" />

export async function* streamResponse(
  response: Response,
): AsyncGenerator<string, void, undefined> {
  if (response.status !== 200) {
    throw new Error(await response.text());
  }

  if (!response.body) {
    throw new Error("No response body returned.");
  }

  // Use ReadableSteam constructor instead of Readable.from() function
  const body = response.body;
  const stream = new ReadableStream<Uint8Array>({
    async start(controller) {
      for await (const chunk of body as any) {
        controller.enqueue(chunk);
      }
      controller.close();
    },
  }) as any;

  yield* stream.pipeThrough(new TextDecoderStream("utf-8"));
}
```

@sestinj There's been a few messages on the Discord on this. I can see the pros and cons of both approaches.

By searching for the API keys directly, you might not necessarily catch every single case but you'll catch the majority of them. You'll also catch cases where the keys are stored outside of the `config.json`, for example in a variable in the `config.ts`. By disabling autocomplete in the `config.json`, you'll catch all cases of API keys in the JSON, but then you'll lose the usefulness of the autocomplete in that file. You also won't catch the API keys in other files like the `config.ts`, unless you disable autocomplete in all files in the `.continue` directory, and then you're reducing the usefulness of autocomplete even more.

So instead of force disabling it in the `.configure` directory,  should we make disabling it in the `.continue` directory a config option which is disabled by default, and document the risk of enabling it in the docs? We could either set a default value for `disableInFiles`, or create a new config option. The risk with setting a default value in `disableInFiles` is that users may inadvertently turn it back on again by configuring their own files and not including the continue directory in the array. Although we could document that as well.

