Renaming the parameters helps to avoid issues with variable shadowing and ensures that the correct values are passed to the functions. In JavaScript and TypeScript, if a variable is declared in an outer scope and a variable with the same name is declared in an inner scope, the inner variable shadows the outer one. This can lead to unexpected behavior, such as the outer variable being undefined when accessed within the inner scope.

I know the video is cut down as much as possible but you see two debug sessions. The first one shows distinct set of parameters, which prevents any accidental shadowing and makes the code easier to understand and maintain. So you see with the renamed ags, the value is correctly set, and gets forwarded to the mcp server as expected. Then you see that I revert my changes, meaning back to each functions having same args naming, you see that args is not set in that context, so there are no arguments that can be handed over to the mcp server which is the reason for the error shown at the end of the video.

